		<div class="toolbar"><p>{finish==='velvet'?'Brush one way, then the other. Your mark lives inside the stationary word.':'Sweep through the letters to polish a glossy trail. Turn the light: the reflection changes, not the ink.'}</p><div><button onclick={()=>surface?.demo()} disabled={!available}>Brush the word <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></button><button onclick={()=>surface?.reset()} disabled={!available}>Reset material <span aria-hidden="true">↺</span></button></div></div>
		<div class="controls"><label><span>Move the light</span><input type="range" min="0" max="360" step="1" bind:value={light} disabled={!available} /><output>{light}°</output></label><label class="baseline"><input type="checkbox" bind:checked={baseline} />Static baseline</label><label class="baseline"><input type="checkbox" bind:checked={bending} />Let the word bend</label></div>
	</section>
	<div class="colophon"><p>Still a word.<br /><em>Now a small experience.</em></p><details><summary>Under the surface <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Velvet stores signed pile direction. The glaze stores a separate polishing field: a stroke reduces micro-roughness, narrowing a reflected area-light stripe. The model keeps that stripe’s one-dimensional integrated light constant as its width changes; it does not paint a color trail. Both materials keep the native letter outline fixed by default. Bending is a separate optional experiment.</p><p>The fine finish uses twelve fixed-phase, physical-pixel-filtered material waves inspired by <a href="https://visualcomputing.ist.ac.at/publications/2026/DeformableTextures/">Kalinov and colleagues’ 2026 spectrum-rescaling research</a>. Optional bending changes their apparent spectrum while keeping phases attached to the material. This is a finite-band illustration, not their full adaptive method, a fluid solver, a calibrated polishing process or a complete reflection model.</p><p>The static baseline removes the material history without erasing it. Reset restores both finishes. Reduced-motion controls update a still specimen. Layout changes remap the normalized sheet to the new heading; DPR-only changes replace native coverage without resetting nap or polish. Unsupported source/output budgets show a static fallback.</p></details></div>
	<footer><a href="/lab/interfaces">All material studies <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><p>Meaning in the letter. Feeling in the surface.</p></footer>
